\appendixsection{Setting Up the ADC and DAC in a Linux Environment}
\label{sec:ADC_DAC_linux_commands}

After the computer is powered off, restarted, or rebooted, several commands must be executed before the ADC and DAC APIs can be used. The first step is to obtain superuser privileges. For the \texttt{tmoon} account, this is accomplished using the following command:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
sudo su
\end{lstlisting}

When prompted, enter the password associated with the \texttt{tmoon} account:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
[sudo] password for tmoon: shoelace
\end{lstlisting}

After obtaining superuser privileges, the directories containing the ADC and DAC driver files must be accessed. The following commands initialize the ADC and DAC drivers, respectively.

For the ADC driver, navigate to the ADC driver directory and execute the following commands:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
cd /usr/src/linux/drivers/16ai32ssc/driver
make clean
make all
./start
\end{lstlisting}

For the DAC driver, navigate to the DAC driver directory and execute the same commands:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
cd /usr/src/linux/drivers/16ao16/driver
make clean
make all
./start
\end{lstlisting}

The commands above must be executed in both the ADC and DAC driver directories. Failure to initialize either driver may result in errors when attempting to use the ADC or DAC APIs.

After these steps have been completed for both drivers, the ADC and DAC APIs are initialized and ready for use.

\appendixsection{How to Use the Code}
\label{sec:how_to_use_code}

From the home directory of the \texttt{tmoon} account, navigate to the directory containing the real-time array processing code using the following command:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
cd /Documents/Nathan/code/real_time_array_processing
\end{lstlisting}

The following files are the primary source files used during the experiments:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
main.c
main.h
Continuous_reading.c
makefile
pmatlib.h
pmatlib.c
\end{lstlisting}

The files \texttt{main.c}, \texttt{main.h}, and \texttt{Continuous\_reading.c} contain the primary code used to configure and execute the experiments and are the main files that may need to be modified when changing the experimental configuration. The \texttt{pmatlib.h} and \texttt{pmatlib.c} files contain supporting functions used by the program and generally do not need to be modified.

The \texttt{makefile} should only need to be modified when an additional source file is added to the program. If a new source file is added, the appropriate source-file list in the \texttt{makefile} must be updated so that the new file is included during compilation.

To compile the program and run the selected test, execute the following commands:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=bash]
make all
./continuous_reading
\end{lstlisting}

The \texttt{make all} command compiles the source code and generates the executable. The \texttt{./continuous\_reading} command then executes the compiled program using the configuration specified in the source files.

The following sections describe the purpose of each of the primary files and explain which portions of the code should be modified when configuring the system for different experiments.

\appendixsection{Supporting Functions}
\label{sec:supplemental_code_base}

This appendix presents selected functions from the main source file, \texttt{Continuous\_reading.c}, that support the implementation of the adaptive filtering and array-processing algorithms described in this thesis. The complete source file contains additional hardware configuration, data acquisition, and supporting functions that are not reproduced here. The functions included in this appendix were selected because they illustrate the principal computational steps of the proposed system.

The following function implements the LMS update step used for adaptive filtering. The filter coefficients are updated using the error signal, the step size, and the corresponding samples stored in the filter buffer.

\lstset{
    language=C,
    basicstyle=\ttfamily\small,
    breaklines=true,
    breakatwhitespace=false,
    columns=fullflexible,
    keepspaces=true
}

\begin{lstlisting}[language=C]
void update_filter(double* filter, double* filter_buffer, double error, double step_size, int length){
  for(int i = 0; i < length; i++){
    filter[i] = filter[i] + step_size * error * filter_buffer[i];
  }
}
\end{lstlisting}

The following function implements the FIR filtering operation. The input buffer is shifted to make room for the newest sample, after which the filter output is calculated as the sum of the products between the filter coefficients and the corresponding buffer samples.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=C]
double FIR_filtering(double new_input, double* filter, double* filter_buffer, int filter_length){
   double sum = 0;

   for(int i = filter_length - 1; i > 0; i--){
      filter_buffer[i] = filter_buffer[i - 1];
      sum += filter[i] * filter_buffer[i];
   }
   filter_buffer[0] = new_input;
   sum += filter[0] * filter_buffer[0];

   return(sum);
}
\end{lstlisting}

The following functions implement the fractional-delay filters used in the array-processing algorithm. The function \texttt{build\_filters()} calculates the propagation delay between the source position and each microphone position. The maximum propagation delay is then used as a reference to determine the relative delay for each sensor. This allows the individual sensor signals to be aligned to a common reference.

The relative delay is separated into an integer-sample delay and a fractional-sample delay. The integer portion is implemented using leading zeros, while the fractional portion is implemented using a truncated sinc filter. The resulting filter therefore combines the bulk delay and fractional delay into a single filter. The filter length is determined by the integer delay and the interpolation length, where $P$ defines the half-length of the sinc filter.

The function \texttt{delaysinc()} computes the fractional-delay sinc coefficients. The parameter $D$ defines the spacing between adjacent microphones.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=C]
void build_filters(double x_pos, double y_pos, double** filter_bank, u32* filter_length, double** filter_buffer, int num_sensors, float start_pos){
   double tau[num_sensors];
   double taup;
   double mic_pos = start_pos;
   double max = 0;

   for(int i = 0; i < num_sensors; i++){
      tau[i] = sqrt((mic_pos - x_pos)*(mic_pos - x_pos)+(-y_pos)*(-y_pos))/C;

      if (tau[i] > max){
         max = tau[i];
      }
      mic_pos += D;
   }

/* Build combined delay + fractional-delay filter */
   for (int i = 0; i < num_sensors; i++){
      taup = max - tau[i];

      double nTs = taup * Fs;
      int nTsint = floor(nTs);

      CALLOCVECTOR(filter_buffer[i], double,nTsint + 2 * P + 1);
      filter_length[i] = nTsint + 2 * P + 1;
      delaysinc(taup, filter_bank[i]);
   }
}

void delaysinc(double taup, double* filter){
   double nTs = taup * Fs;
   int nTsint = floor(nTs);
   double nTsfrac = nTs - nTsint;
   taup = nTsfrac * Ts;

   double tlist[2 * P + 1];

   if(nTsint != 0){
      for(int i = 0; i < nTsint; i++)
         filter[i] = 0;
   }

   for(int i = 0; i < 2 * P + 1; i++){
      tlist[i] = (((double)(i - P) / Fs) - taup)* B * 2 * M_PI;
   }

/*
 * Store the sinc filter immediately after the
 * integer-delay zeros.
 */
   for(int i = 0; i < 2 * P + 1; i++){
      if(tlist[i] != 0){
         filter[nTsint + i] = sin(tlist[i]) / tlist[i];
      }
      else{
        filter[nTsint + i] = 1.0;
      }
   }
}
\end{lstlisting}

